          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "After starting or changing a blood pressure medicine, guidelines recommend reassessment at roughly monthly intervals until the blood pressure target is reached. Once readings are stable, follow-up reviews are recommended every three to six months. This guidance comes from major cardiology society consensus guidance, specifically outlined in \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" and \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
